b/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "576083d0-69bc-5a44-8497-3c93280947cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-181600" :question/problem "![](images/83/83ebd6f5d742a869b267a0cead4a9a94ae17fa534ca3e1cfddbb495c67390ecf.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198476 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89059596-c90e-5800-b5c8-67b3cfc816fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383635} {:answer/id #uuid "12087dc5-970c-5c09-a6ed-0645ee25d2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383636} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb1972c-1680-542d-ace5-14a31ee17f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/