uid "393bef1a-778c-53b8-a73e-75ffc95e3405" :question/math-academy-id "q-181958" :question/problem "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A=\\{p,q\\}$ and $B=\\{w,z\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/6b/6baae52e45d00d31eac732a23be655b648765c27cfcdc667c511b176d5bbbc88.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/38e9d12b0c09ae066149d95235a6918712214edc147c57775da2d880b584cb9e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c52fa43-923f-576a-ba23-dee22206f178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9adb78f087651508a43514756abbc45170766d6bb14a069053c4bf71e78e0a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389911} {:answer/id #uuid "ae96fe3d-4eb2-5715-aab5-91382649a4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d42f10aac16e2c7405fbf158dacb1b46137cc03800f8660d0ff4cfe11c9edc63.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389912} {:answer/id #uuid "2db12bff-09e6-5312-965d-054d7f6b4fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389913} {:answer/id #uuid "6f56907b-5366-5f06-9fae-bd37aae2b8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b6a2c5b3c9fdded8d615980df00228e2637515af0a23f62ae58b891bec3e3e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389914} {:answer/id #uuid "5924e649-be02-5520-9d5e-94f6d2c07e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a1b02e904c1df760f47d623d3ff17538c284d7ef6125b786165113a383c79c3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db12bff-09e6-5312-965d-054d7f6b4fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389913} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa6976a-3c32-54c7-bedf-912fa5e61648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9854e016-8489-5582-ad5c-ffa3a5b6e5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-181959" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/17/179fdaa3921cb29f2d25ff97bce262fd13fb2ff591c12e97a33953e95967f6fc.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/73/7399e42988df8a809013393d8873b6a6e5f60b64c0419eadd7bb3c334eb410d6.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $N\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/16/16ff13c793d3c3f57eefee638fbc1ff83569ef6d084d9d053f8585f72c8812d7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1204bd88-b470-5b91-b7c2-a8d4ad537898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/463ae2387423a48dd557af8cfd9b01b6bc08b95f1b3c2f337c6b0c53d3731fa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389918} {:answer/id #uuid "60caae02-3071-514b-9258-9f96b6664d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389919} {:answer/id #uuid "746ae67c-caa3-593c-9e3f-51831b036f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} {:answer/id #uuid "c89a44bf-6d27-578e-9136-6babbb95e931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f28e6f8c8e6e9fa13b5285e25e5460d26548ffd9d71077d1c6073e6c4c743a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389921} {:answer/id #uuid "51564c2b-bcad-5e6e-9c34-dbd7a16d3a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/5888cf137eea674995b4c9cc37285b4130df5de3a2a2fa63b1b5b195910526c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "746ae67c-caa3-593c-9e3f-51831b036f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} :answer-field/id #uuid "c241a74d-c7f4-5b96-a451-f6a50f08f04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bae802a-d591-53eb-b628-21b012d03cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-181968" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/4d/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e4b951-13c9-5a36-b192-be0382fd5de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285230} {:answer/id #uuid "38b875e6-8324-5fb8-b44c-1942aec1f630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285320} {:answer/id #uuid "3afc2aac-a7d1-51e8-b9d4-b33f0e5402c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285342} {:answer/id #uuid "49d86e10-16cc-573a-8288-7239e3634d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285471} {:answer/id #uuid "b07ab7da-2fc2-562c-8125-1b5b93bdb1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afc2aac-a7d1-51e8-b9d4-b33f0e5402c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285342} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e25e254-0bd7-5e66-8a1b-42a0fadd9812" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ac2696-6112-5009-83df-a6d2029a30ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285083} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5af158-ca02-5410-8b81-c7390a1cd242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285935} {:answer/id #uuid "aa23d36e-a922-5886-a634-958087a9546b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286337} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bc792c-2254-56d4-ad6d-01066466ad2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286573} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ed6430-0b88-51f8-9bca-633ed8d4f74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286583} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a83d15-2879-5881-9cfa-35d5ccdd77d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2bc792c-2254-56d4-ad6d-01066466ad2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286573} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1bf5748-e93c-5b56-b290-f06e55daa354" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8574c8b5-4403-5982-8ea8-0dec7fa70982" :question/math-academy-id "q-181983" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cce16725dddcfc83098ee2323576b8e01bbc3f2625d17cb7e6030826ad9fbcd8.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,x-1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny<x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d6c485-e050-55c8-9679-fd6d97aa00e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96a29779da824d7d2dc7b6d4f2b9c781a4a593b58e44f491724221c77888e3e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389925} {:answer/id #uuid "08a4e04e-4888-5403-95d8-f078acf2c991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5b130683075dd04e4b5129065d32b4d75b3dabfaff0b05b90de858b5e0f2961.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389926} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e8e5e1-7a36-52d6-b8df-aa671398b7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389927} {:answer/id #uuid "66761d80-0b06-5258-89e9-adc40f67af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/288ca0010ecc1fada05a67637b583a6e86b7f805d4e272e91563a13c18fc164a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389928} {:answer/id #uuid "09687f1b-acf3-52b9-85d2-f72a782bfbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/3332464bdc0583c6ce99bf126805abaf57461e67aee6bdf12c95e11042baf9c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e8e5e1-7a36-52d6-b8df-aa671398b7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389927} :answer-field/id #uuid "46ec56ed-6fc0-52ed-aa83-998bc8081175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c70aae50-ffb5-59f2-930c-cbbe5d9bb642" :question/math-academy-id "q-181985" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=[-1,0]\\times [-1,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,0]\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,0],\\,y\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c93f3ad399b88ccbaac990f8f73e93cfdcb1192f52b9b12f2d3ce178776fa13b.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,0]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,0],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/65/6557201d202a401a92ee37c9a69eab9ab6ab87c6342dbb9d2afebae30141346f.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,0]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/97/97a74b119ddb8a618d6564bc077d03a5a4ceb7152702beb3682f2eab1a436b70.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "167ee290-c682-5fed-ba58-a3f8ff1bfa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284992} {:answer/id #uuid "5b353c2e-5d30-5a8e-aba9-8867e5be727f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285622} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1158a7-b4ae-51b5-8786-b3f74e91071b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285658} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff29248-215b-5815-a8ee-4db3139b66ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285965} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42a5c1-8d53-56c1-8ccc-0e00f3246008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286371} {:answer/id #uuid "f36669c5-1dfc-578b-9980-2f5d1be54bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad42a5c1-8d53-56c1-8ccc-0e00f3246008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286371} :answer-field/id #uuid "1294729d-a5bd-5c49-b0c6-f422f0a1974b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3256dce2-fbdc-53bb-94fb-b50be06f7aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285247} {:answer/id #uuid "365e5097-c020-5b22-b68b-4975c0accf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285291} {:answer/id #uuid "4a542a38-c9d6-54cd-a3a5-14ee13c3b045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285477} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe1bd3c-03a3-5cf6-bb44-8377edab20aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286244} {:answer/id #uuid "f5276376-3983-56b0-9dcb-7aa01a1fa68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5276376-3983-56b0-9dcb-7aa01a1fa68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287033} :answer-field/id #uuid "43159098-cae7-5334-ac8d-6d7721f57f39" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0067ce51-137c-57cd-8c4c-7e69e7b8398e" :question/math-academy-id "q-182016" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f54c771d1b7045bbaa301c9d595a45dc64fbdfc6b1c83c152fb6fc892373ea06.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dcce5b4-ad09-55c5-9cf9-45359eb47f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No interval exists where ${f}^{′}(x)<0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285370} {:answer/id #uuid "4860a783-68be-5547-ac44-eb08093b8475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285453} {:answer/id #uuid "72e858e1-0117-5a93-9463-da04318f6c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,\\infty )$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285847} {:answer/id #uuid "75858322-27d2-528d-94c9-d4ee6c9a1e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285878} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfecc1f-1a89-593f-ad1e-f221c7f84c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75858322-27d2-528d-94c9-d4ee6c9a1e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285878} :answer-field/id #uuid "9249e33e-0da2-550c-bd1e-2a7bf97f1f67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c0a1416-8923-507f-b1c1-0e9a65b61f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-18227" :question/problem "![](images/26/269b71c2ec5653b6577e904a43374a45a0830f671850c7157a8d1bbfb49308f3.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401690} {:answer/id #uuid "13957928-5c88-587e-8787-1039aba9a145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401691} {:answer/id #uuid "23eb6ae8-6643-5983-b5dd-d782a53c5d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401692} {:answer/id #uuid "daaad303-eb94-54dc-b583-e74bb50bf666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $k=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401693} {:answer/id #uuid "01dc8965-c2ae-5ff0-b5e5-19a4d4675483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=10$ and $k=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401690} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6e33e7-dd66-569c-9ddd-b087772632c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a62d5fa-3b38-507f-b4fe-dfd19836563a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18244" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 11 & 9 & k+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 11 & ∗ & k+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${a}_{22}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2k\\cdot (k+3)-11\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+6k-66 & =-70 \\\\ 2{k}^{2}+6k+4 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}+3k+2 & =0 \\\\ (k+1)(k+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "922efb98-094c-5cb0-b598-ca53dbd40c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 4$ and $t = 5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057213} {:answer/id #uuid "cc82bc67-8e72-5ecb-9f14-69a51bf471c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057214} {:answer/id #uuid "08522422-373c-50b5-8818-1a41c69958ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t =-5$ and $t = 4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057215} {:answer/id #uuid "3edcdacf-1a99-5d1f-8af8-bf61ed390ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t =-5$ and $t =-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057216} {:answer/id #uuid "78eb832a-251e-55b3-8f40-495326995efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t =-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057217}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8d8e5be9-e228-55ee-8e70-778d9a594d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c22e6-8533-526d-adfc-b7173bb287ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401717} {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401718} {:answer/id #uuid "f493d638-8bce-58ac-912a-5338bcaaef9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401719} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9478e1-7ffc-5682-8c4f-871cb83d3a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401720} {:answer/id #uuid "98c916e5-ba01-54de-ae08-6a77aed9f1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401718} :answer-field/id #uuid "5695dc0f-925d-5609-ad66-b2dab30bc5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c6851eb-a487-5113-99b9-8f1c04863168" :question/math-academy-id "q-18246" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04828dd4-f030-5633-b9ab-2ca90b202225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284827} {:answer/id #uuid "10872bea-02dc-53b8-8a81-d21f0443ee2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284932} {:answer/id #uuid "28dc2ce6-543c-571a-a921-bb628ebdeb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285145} {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "827cdff1-9d64-55e9-b43a-8f9a6a5b14e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f7dafc-fd39-5a57-8833-37de29245686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf370fc0-22a1-5807-80d7-314f76c5d442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "627e7a3d-46e0-5ce8-b4f0-d14bd806cb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-18256" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}]$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{aligned}| & =4|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ y & -5\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-3y & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-3y & -2 \\\\ 2 & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (10-y)-2\\cdot (15y-2)+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =40-4y-30y+4-9{y}^{2}+12 \\\\  & =-9{y}^{2}-34y+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $81,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{y}^{2}-34y+56 & =81 \\\\ -9{y}^{2}-34y-25 & =0 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+34y+25 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{{34}^{2}-4\\cdot 9\\cdot 25}}{2\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{256}}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm 16}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-17\\pm 8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f06230e-8816-51ce-b392-2abd455f3b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285208} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8539bc-f4a1-50db-90f6-0b45e3ceff04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285517} {:answer/id #uuid "52f9a129-aa6f-59fe-9748-78c62d9d1a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285550} {:answer/id #uuid "d79adfbb-4209-5e76-80b7-c3a2f369dbcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286751} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b8e647-e565-5c04-9e76-013451742246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e8539bc-f4a1-50db-90f6-0b45e3ceff04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285517} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec0d804-b8fa-5b33-8525-d5694c64c34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce77ea41-d1eb-55f8-914d-e1a94a42a6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-18279" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7576155e-753f-578e-a4ec-c1e146863345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285876} {:answer/id #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a448-efa8-5e13-8fb2-e9929ad565e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286140} {:answer/id #uuid "e6986340-6e6f-5163-a46c-b7ce43a43001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286898} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4f3922-637f-578e-9c1d-529afc788b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7804ba9f-5287-5069-b31b-fbfc92859355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285906} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f3d5af-0a6c-5449-9acd-f6ef2a68a144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "882c4c5b-7c6a-5276-989d-a6c4749ae87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18280" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e56ee5-4fb6-5816-869f-e39c23a78b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285084} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbf79ad-839a-5229-93e9-65fad5cfd1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285385} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb285a6-7afd-5ad8-ab82-98a513400c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cea90f-f0ed-5d57-bca3-334408a81e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287013} {:answer/id #uuid "fd525363-2a62-5343-aac2-348e4bfefb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd525363-2a62-5343-aac2-348e4bfefb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287105} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac120b1e-d324-518f-9e93-f08acaa655f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "670b2139-910a-54c1-b1cc-9c11eb5fdc1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18284" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-16.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f415776-37dc-54e3-9800-2d01e510bf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284919} {:answer/id #uuid "60a2a07e-a39e-5566-8bba-7375b15d76ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285675} {:answer/id #uuid "7595df60-c82c-590b-b868-221b7368f2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285879} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab4c0e-9b0e-5237-9348-d4be45093c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286252} {:answer/id #uuid "f133bf14-3c6d-5286-a6d1-bf00852e4721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab4c0e-9b0e-5237-9348-d4be45093c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286252} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa7a81a-d6ae-5365-9fa0-1142a694af59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5cade28-be41-57ee-910f-46e652127d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-18285" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232327fd-1dfa-5996-8e84-95bb6a9f8cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285103} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f41583-27a2-56ac-9a18-17faf2b31721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286592} {:answer/id #uuid "c776e656-4613-5245-90ac-bb7e8e9dd6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286614} {:answer/id #uuid "d31191b1-ed1c-5824-a66f-2602858ec4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286707} {:answer/id #uuid "ef342a1d-6164-5836-994c-98cb5484b941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31191b1-ed1c-5824-a66f-2602858ec4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286707} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c346e23-1fbd-5f16-925c-878fb4d2e5f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcb03ff4-bc8a-5928-846f-20e56388fdb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18286" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $39.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}=\\frac{1}{39}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{39}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aba01f9-7f51-5a8f-a7a8-dc4fa8fb483a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285161} {:answer/id #uuid "51897cf6-89ff-547a-a77d-fb4c229f6e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285537} {:answer/id #uuid "83757217-5865-5baa-8ad8-1b2c54dde4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285995} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f44981-7ac4-5b60-aa71-18f4807ae1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286563} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e75515-8616-5fe3-a6a1-8dc2e1fa3a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f44981-7ac4-5b60-aa71-18f4807ae1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286563} :answer-field/id #uuid "909dfb56-2abf-58f1-9f5d-3fcdb81ce8b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b862f7e3-dfeb-537e-b5d6-1de6a752faf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-18288" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $71.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-3-10) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{71}\\cdot 13=\\frac{13}{71}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452ce30f-4585-5498-a993-5b217e1d0196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{16.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285419} {:answer/id #uuid "49a22484-c3da-5cec-b7da-479586d8d217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285466} {:answer/id #uuid "96439185-b8dc-52e9-937e-957a9cb1e0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{146.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286152} {:answer/id #uuid "ced085f4-6256-5d5f-9620-f202f6465752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286664} {:answer/id #uuid "d926ea56-38a8-5589-a381-e5f63ca7cc1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced085f4-6256-5d5f-9620-f202f6465752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286664} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5adac8-1b4d-53ee-a9c1-9b496bd2433c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23a2df8e-948e-5b82-a7dc-fb5d528e3df5" :question/math-academy-id "q-18320" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6+7\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{53}\\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{1961}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{1961}})≈{64.6}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b7cee0-d64b-5acf-80f6-e4395feda439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285016} {:answer/id #uuid "43074725-3f05-5199-8c89-774b6421cd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285407} {:answer/id #uuid "aad0caa3-f123-5a0b-bd2b-e7efddced7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286346} {:answer/id #uuid "e3226fe7-e48a-5225-9072-a624a09264f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286863} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ca23d4-64d5-5790-abdd-f668d2d603e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43074725-3f05-5199-8c89-774b6421cd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285407} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c5b613-158c-53cc-a454-1a0b462e7843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c5361cf-1bb5-5b1a-8111-4c0f01c39896" :question/math-academy-id "q-18372" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-2,0,0⟩$ and $d=⟨4,-1,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198467 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27018e18-9368-5171-ae78-097adc8bcddf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646e83b-57f6-5ecf-a75b-7c4a9c405f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284839} {:answer/id #uuid "3692de82-6368-50f4-85a8-79e3f1e8c998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285295} {:answer/id #uuid "3d257612-0090-576d-a600-50cbe6bad651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285365} {:answer/id #uuid "a09ba867-8ec3-54d4-8931-a971f581f822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286251} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6835bc-dc1d-5ba3-9812-a7ad1bb22643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a09ba867-8ec3-54d4-8931-a971f581f822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286251} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c40065-325c-548d-9311-e10440b73b94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f917453-594e-5ed0-997b-868645389d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-183736" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z>2.31).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f25b8fea7403579d1ee40cc00b9447e3362dc28c2e9d41f762f51df5e11daaf8.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>2.31).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e01ca4fd737cea9afe95374a17c0428f83a6717d98a7557222a856aa2d0c02.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<-2.31).$\n\n![](images/ce/ce206061194e357b9cd75f9b260e4035a8600726a17cc0ee04ab39219bd45e94.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>2.31)=P(Z<-2.31)=Φ(-2.31)=0.0104.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38804ecb-a9e5-5292-83cd-22d2f3dddc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285314} {:answer/id #uuid "87af4911-57b8-5d1b-a61c-48885ecb8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286030} {:answer/id #uuid "b25bb67f-9d56-52b5-a4e4-2d21207798bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286419} {:answer/id #uuid "b647e886-213b-5e34-8574-a0e95f37c1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286452} {:answer/id #uuid "db777d82-845a-5914-94e9-dcb99836452f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db777d82-845a-5914-94e9-dcb99836452f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286786} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc839666-75c3-59d3-a3a7-4e1a2451ad41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e48bd53-4841-5a1d-adee-85eb3b590727" :question/math-academy-id "q-18374" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩$ and $q=⟨\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{3},1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}+1\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d2757f-1179-5ad6-b8c7-5165ac3a3eff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3b8687-5d97-544f-aa1a-86c0b2bd616d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2886" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284918} {:answer/id #uuid "11576bff-6a41-572f-92ae-e0901e0d6cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284940} {:answer/id #uuid "4385ad3b-c6a8-56d7-ada9-c88d8fc45a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285411} {:answer/id #uuid "56cc73aa-3f8f-5571-a2a4-b6a713f9f063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285586} {:answer/id #uuid "df0ece69-cb89-59f2-b91c-2ba3dd2806fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56cc73aa-3f8f-5571-a2a4-b6a713f9f063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285586} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d0887f-0f37-5d26-9b95-3a68607bab32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ba6a2e8-d87f-530d-9d04-188512f760d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-183823" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-0.18<Z<0.18).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.3974\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ | $\\,0.3897\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4364\\,$ | $\\,0.4325\\,$ | $\\,0.4286\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.18<Z<0.18).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/06/061aec33cb30172ec64c89841fc70cb2e5bec9b89f92ef0427ff9673bfa99219.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.18<Z<0.18) & =P(Z<0.18)-P(Z\\le -0.18) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.18)-P(Z\\le -0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.18)=0.4286,\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $Φ(0.18).$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.18) & =P(Z\\le 0.18) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.18) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.18) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & =1-0.4286 \\\\  & =0.5714\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.18<Z<0.18) & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & =0.5714-0.4286 \\\\  & =0.1428.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607f5fc0-710c-50ec-853a-f1cbe588f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390328} {:answer/id #uuid "f8387a2d-e7fd-51de-b176-ea56d9e540b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2eec72a5444533c1bb4f86836cf8479b76ae38d69c35f5f5eef5fd313a2365f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390329} {:answer/id #uuid "36db9686-690f-54ec-bfac-28a409caed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01d87db018bcbbf013d7f33519418849fe70e0d772870335482a7b07cfc89fd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390330} {:answer/id #uuid "c165510a-291f-5076-b75f-5f046b3f04a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70f7c02b3bca2b816858eaef766b5bbf3ced8d8abf101ef38ecda19b73781ee3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390331} {:answer/id #uuid "9f87f4ca-c61a-584a-8253-8ab30a3d9929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa3411e662566a609de5163269dbc1e7c0ad92f415b52936c5ef568d7058d2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607f5fc0-710c-50ec-853a-f1cbe588f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390328} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a31ac2-1521-5379-9c4e-3e1335f990ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659e44f3-afb3-574d-a533-7467d2366762" :question/math-academy-id "q-184489" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-1,-3),(-1,1),(2,-3),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc5f1e81-abf5-50d0-a56a-6bbee4a28f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86cd6fb9fd52708dd41257a7df95db869ce6638e0dde902e2f7847d09021b530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390335} {:answer/id #uuid "542c23bb-867d-5c38-8c44-6f9ba3f17e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a713f172fca69aa423ae4664f2a6e31eb4315b666f17b9ea4a7c1ffbc8322be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390336} {:answer/id #uuid "8d51a496-cf22-5d8a-bc7b-ea2b23da875a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/748c2e450098f81b24d22a34ce375b1ea365c4879a33bfb2a539065e9e800e15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390337} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb92f3f-a231-5381-be9f-dc081efff8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390338} {:answer/id #uuid "6bea186d-febc-581c-a875-42e213a2d2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d714297c6acc363f55a775fb3f6007145dec3aa415476d5c758f8c2def679ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb92f3f-a231-5381-be9f-dc081efff8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390338} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc74910-d822-50ea-be12-c27bbc4353b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05268f20-60a4-56fb-b13c-c24e63cbe9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-184505" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}={0.5}\\times [0,0.125]={(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](images/47/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203dab4a-3058-5e0e-90fa-52dcc35d2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285078} {:answer/id #uuid "46da0d85-3682-54d0-b58f-5f7b61e746ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x+4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} {:answer/id #uuid "66e054b3-95bb-5323-86e6-76b98c6154cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285725} {:answer/id #uuid "92c0eef2-67dc-58fb-baa0-cc77ea56c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a13dae-3ed1-5b21-b23c-46bc3ddb17e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3974f0a3-ea97-56a9-a9e2-a7e59e1e84b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-1847" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x)=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x) & =4(-sin⁡x)+2(cos⁡x) \\\\  & =2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933cab4-6c6f-5e1e-b9e9-375a48b8bbd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390342} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7f842-ec45-57a8-93f4-8edce627ac5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/3357b2cf67b40afa54b081c67855f28a74d71209459382c0e421b6e2da7d114f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390343} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f47ff-1aa3-50e2-840e-6408b7c4640f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e802fe0b5558c1a076f65da60db5a24a58f667.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390344} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4a02-ebd9-56ca-9cfc-e2db41cc39a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3d7ea5224600381e2dd4f62618ce8a60804f89bb6949b82989017aa2556f87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff039e87-4af3-51fe-96a4-b9dddf2e393f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09d1de29-e974-591a-b590-9887d91abf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18523" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4dacecba4626f7c202777cad0a723295a2954f4b0c65fde83a81c6c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5446db9d-f32b-503a-98c3-71bd4b13a940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9{x}^{1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285562} {:answer/id #uuid "72b849ec-c73e-51c6-8bd6-7d57955bf8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285843} {:answer/id #uuid "93d4bb13-717f-50aa-afa5-f6147b1c9f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-9{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286135} {:answer/id #uuid "a7da2020-89e3-5d5f-bbdb-6fb669e629c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9{x}^{-1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286320} {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320dfd3-7a24-5485-a9b9-30a0c49ac165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76292ca7-955e-5da2-801e-cf1602a2e16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1856" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-3\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{(3/4-1)} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-3}-\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{-1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a56ae7a-dc8e-5f78-b8ad-3af4f9363a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285159} {:answer/id #uuid "37adca1a-8c05-52ee-ba16-5fb2f703f19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285310} {:answer/id #uuid "46961755-2102-5159-b6f1-3f491514e632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285436} {:answer/id #uuid "81352ae6-21e2-5a29-9cfd-846a353cd9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285976} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd5fb69-9d5a-5e58-89b2-6ac6482d75a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81352ae6-21e2-5a29-9cfd-846a353cd9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285976} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ec6303-b1ee-53c3-818d-f60157542720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7085b9d4-0246-5cfc-a6d8-e11f13c66dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-18603" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4585c2a9cf0a66da02f96a17dad5986ed8c401a844276f08b367eed765c111d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/29/299b996bf08e4b4e8560d013829ddd1f42751767cd3f00dcd04c23c95f2c62b4.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{3-(-2)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-2)}{3-(-2)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fa7731-5618-5ce9-aaa9-7b03ecd1aa14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284973} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9d3037-e022-5ee9-86c6-4a652fdbd89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "908" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285519} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef78bd-1cb1-5f6e-a9e2-2847e40b1ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dc8f5f-aa43-50c9-985b-3766b997f085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "702" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287020} {:answer/id #uuid "f7723b12-7830-5ad5-986d-2d31cb3c6493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3dc8f5f-aa43-50c9-985b-3766b997f085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "702" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287020} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d06103-b698-5c91-a0d6-a8c226633dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2ca0dd6-55a4-5e14-9378-5d6846fa942b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18621" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ in the expansion of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13},$ we have $a=3,$ $b={x}^{2},$ and $n=13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}=(\\frac{13}{0}){3}^{13}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+(\\frac{13}{1}){3}^{13-1}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+(\\frac{13}{2}){3}^{13-2}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{13}{13}){3}^{13-13}{({x}^{2})}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{22}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11} & =78\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =78\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =702{x}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ is $702.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d28906-8490-5c29-b464-0d5c1a48004e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} {:answer/id #uuid "99a94d50-1eb7-5245-b930-e867aafb297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286196} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9888fc-dc7e-5ae3-99b6-a76b342aa777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286217} {:answer/id #uuid "cba56c64-acf9-5648-abc7-affc33124f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286645} {:answer/id #uuid "d952dfee-02a8-55ff-a0dc-3cc35185c0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,868" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a94d50-1eb7-5245-b930-e867aafb297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286196} :answer-field/id #uuid "90078a64-d272-59e6-bb4a-64d828c75a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fe2343c-e168-571c-8f87-3172e7d9973d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18624" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ in the expansion of ${(2+x)}^{15}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+x)}^{15},$ we have $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{15}=(\\frac{15}{0}){2}^{15}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{15}{1}){2}^{15-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{15}{2}){2}^{15-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{15}{15}){2}^{15-15}{x}^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{12}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12}{x}^{12}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12} & =455\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =455\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ is $3\\,640.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a39cb08-b579-5a48-a70b-56ed38fa7c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285022} {:answer/id #uuid "3662dd06-672a-55c4-b8a5-1f114830ec13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285292} {:answer/id #uuid "7d872dfb-a0b1-5bbe-85f0-77928067084a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285942} {:answer/id #uuid "dff82d24-8d6e-5267-936b-56f9803096de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286829} {:answer/id #uuid "e05125b1-b319-55eb-a696-0b12a6d73055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05125b1-b319-55eb-a696-0b12a6d73055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286831} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb1f5b4-15ac-5534-aeb5-ffcb51eeab93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72f9bd31-58da-5cc4-b44f-c7d388daa64d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18658" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $4a\\cdot (-b\\times c)=12.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a\\cdot (-b\\times c) & =12 \\\\ 4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =12 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} {:answer/id #uuid "874eb524-3ed8-5726-b354-81594d6ba038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286028} {:answer/id #uuid "8da77226-b79f-5aa1-9fbb-eff1ddcce302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286083} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a3fa5-07d3-5f60-9264-81029b284219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "e77beded-c213-529c-9af4-9172511b2a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e490dbf9-e5ff-5dfa-a559-074583566e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c397f249-716c-58ab-a9bf-91bfc3f7d868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.5,\\,b=-1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401800} {:answer/id #uuid "2dec0c3e-7449-5041-96b3-12d8ef936e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.5,\\,b=2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401801} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401802} {:answer/id #uuid "3da6187f-1bd3-5425-a6fa-9cc0e764d299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1.2,\\,b=0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401803} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8550aa-5225-5217-b55a-8b4d6c0d09f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.2,\\,b=1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401802} :answer-field/id #uuid "4994c6c6-b044-5fa6-ae9d-9a350ba14b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8748ba37-2d9a-5451-b497-621aa22378b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18688" :question/problem "Find the solution of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 &  \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 15a+5b=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=2a-1\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $a,$ we can substitute $b=2a-1$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a+5(2a-1) & =25 \\\\ 15a+10a-5 & =25 \\\\ 25a & =30 \\\\ a & =\\frac{30}{25} \\\\ a & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1.2$ into the  first equation to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2(1.2)-1 \\\\  & =2.4-1 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1.2$ and $b=1.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc62d8df-450f-5f7a-b647-b53c051f8339" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e87017a-3d71-526f-882c-4c2dfef523bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285378} {:answer/id #uuid "779e6eca-f602-5727-b029-1c18d87c1280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285898} {:answer/id #uuid "77d04a2c-eb10-5431-830c-d23e900376e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285903} {:answer/id #uuid "8a09092a-f7a8-583a-ab31-352d4e305731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286049} {:answer/id #uuid "d485a384-181e-5a8e-9b1f-2a7c89a1bcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d04a2c-eb10-5431-830c-d23e900376e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285903} :answer-field/id #uuid "8315d25a-73c2-509a-9efb-209d1dae20b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afe3b13e-ab14-5340-83f7-304f398b7445" :question/math-academy-id "q-18720" :question/problem "Let $(p,q)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $p+2q?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equation by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 & |\\times 100 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}36p+120q=36 \\\\ -19p-76q=38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can solve for $p$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19p-76q & =38 \\\\ -19p & =76q+38 \\\\ p & =\\frac{76q+38}{-19} \\\\ p & =-4q-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $q,$ we can substitute $p=-4q-2$ into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36p+120q & =36 \\\\ 36(-4q-2)+120q & =36 \\\\ -144q-72+120q & =36 \\\\ -24q & =108 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{108}{24} \\\\ q & =-4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $q=-4.5$ into the equation $p=-4q-2$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-4q-2 \\\\ p & =-4(-4.5)-2 \\\\ p & =18-2 \\\\ p & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=16$ and $q=-4.5.$ So, \n\n$$\np+2q=16+2(-4.5)=16-9=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18490f60-4edc-508c-abe1-e7e0f13c065d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285003} {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} {:answer/id #uuid "e405d079-ad9d-55f2-b5eb-d4d63e4f65f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb434cbb-a7e4-590f-89bc-63a9f59cf077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286942} {:answer/id #uuid "f66903c6-f94c-504b-bfea-93036d198d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecf3453-145b-5f3a-bf2b-36b631e0d173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78f6b62-d8f4-5963-b2aa-dcc684d0394b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18862" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe61b3a1-9e6d-55b4-b6b7-27c3bed2c392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102483} {:answer/id #uuid "186ff4db-a684-5234-a9fe-d4445b22cdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102484} {:answer/id #uuid "22ad60e1-fb01-562a-88b8-08b42c1e9884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102485} {:answer/id #uuid "28b4bc21-f68e-5747-965e-a89aa5afb118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102486} {:answer/id #uuid "49474f4b-2912-595f-95b0-9e42acee4bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102487}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9edea1ff-b532-59c5-b499-57648c90c425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102482} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390348} {:answer/id #uuid "1a526c30-a852-56c6-b14c-c2025f540361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390349} {:answer/id #uuid "ab385b24-8ef8-5f1a-bd08-498b2099ce33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390350} {:answer/id #uuid "585a8c8c-14a7-54a3-b24f-e4f0fbf0ee6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390351} {:answer/id #uuid "353305b2-1bf3-530e-9fe4-e18cf98ffcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390348} :answer-field/id #uuid "13eb807a-8732-502e-90a4-aea8f6fe280d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46dce84e-4f15-50e3-8511-5dd62cf1728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18975" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,8,3⟩.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}\\cdot ⟨-2,8,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0daa6c22-b6f3-51d9-a6de-9fda6e8596b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284905} {:answer/id #uuid "1abecb7e-2e0a-5388-81ae-f79fa8d93b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285026} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac816ad-f66f-5fc9-a2a3-07c82119d299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82.28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285162} {:answer/id #uuid "69f3be6b-4783-52f4-84fe-9b95d561ffc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{94.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285757} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1020da-a591-5782-8a4a-25069e02b21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{74.66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0daa6c22-b6f3-51d9-a6de-9fda6e8596b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284905} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf80208-e230-50e2-a705-9a72d129b4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "213ad054-3594-5fcc-a936-bdc01a05c42f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19036" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨4,-3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}})≈{85.82}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad90516-6de3-5322-9799-56d50aedcba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284878} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4ce063-2662-501f-a7d4-6cffbba3f19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{110.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285201} {:answer/id #uuid "38a6c708-fe56-5283-a85a-91fad1eaa1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285317} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba26421-66e7-5b20-882a-624c20d91ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285628} {:answer/id #uuid "85ac83f4-5a71-50ed-ae67-c0b78cdc3044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{132.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad90516-6de3-5322-9799-56d50aedcba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284878} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d084ddf-6dab-583a-a6fe-289d9ce77544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "024f1eea-1cf9-519a-baef-cf481604490c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19038" :question/problem "Given that $p\\cdot q=-5$, find the angle between $p=⟨4t,-2,t⟩$ and $q=⟨1,3,-3⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $p\\cdot q=-5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =-5 \\\\ 4t\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 3+t\\cdot (-3) & =-5 \\\\ 4t-6-3t & =-5 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨4(1),-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $p$ and $q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,||\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{399}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTHerefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{399}})≈{104.5}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d15acc-1672-5ec4-bb78-f3474884b897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284830} {:answer/id #uuid "48fabaf5-be8a-589c-8cf0-074c455a9240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458} {:answer/id #uuid "63f73e2e-4004-5fc8-9825-f8337f6c2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285697} {:answer/id #uuid "766bb2aa-4af5-5e09-aed3-a777505c1a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285891} {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8570317-ecab-5ed9-a868-188d2c2ead8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9daa906-697e-5ad9-9a96-96e98679f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-1904" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ if $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-x)+5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2x-5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the derivative and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-5csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "905d1f6a-72aa-5984-8c7d-920497fd58e4" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08847216-8695-5c58-9c55-4efd9ea37cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284855} {:answer/id #uuid "713ca5fb-8006-5933-96ce-17ac96219558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285826} {:answer/id #uuid "75c3ab8f-18e2-59e5-b409-f23279f422cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccotx-\\frac{({x}^{3}+2x)(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285882} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bb6a0e-a3e1-53de-bf1c-a588c28c1165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286254} {:answer/id #uuid "a3628ab9-5c34-5c92-bef6-1314d9e06e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08847216-8695-5c58-9c55-4efd9ea37cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284855} :answer-field/id #uuid "751d5f7c-e24c-5495-bd5f-426fdee41841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd1d008a-84eb-52df-bb09-d41421bc5f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19065" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=({x}^{3}+2x)arccotx.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $arccot$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{3}+2x)arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+2x)+({x}^{3}+2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2)+({x}^{3}+2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5582e8b-3870-5a32-b15e-62b8f8306ce6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions Using the Product or Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1618e03f-9620-5bd5-85cd-1b7993323dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284987} {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} {:answer/id #uuid "35fc1512-0fee-559d-9989-9672ff8d1d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285285} {:answer/id #uuid "5c628087-cf37-51b1-ac2c-fb127ba0396b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285634} {:answer/id #uuid "c234a623-5300-5076-898f-6693292506e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{arctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} :answer-field/id #uuid "418f8f05-ac62-5559-a3d3-33900d47dceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a202483-d8b8-51e4-982b-f5e54acb06af" :question/math-academy-id "q-19067" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)arctan({x}^{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, using the formula for the derivative of $arctanx$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arctan({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064170 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67f41769-a0ff-5ecc-8b05-5890a7ac9fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46152cad-db26-5489-9885-4872107442fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285429} {:answer/id #uuid "729aed06-b282-521d-acb9-4668cd5b0175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arcsec(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285842} {:answer/id #uuid "938eb1ee-7ab4-5c5a-9a1e-41f61385c5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccsc(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286131} {:answer/id #uuid "bacc3b3a-e948-590d-a0b3-c31bb9639de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286488} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0db5b1-a31d-566b-9b38-cd041089c3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arccscx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46152cad-db26-5489-9885-4872107442fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285429} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c584f0-c6ff-5109-ba98-24519ff2f970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ca0aa8-06db-5c6f-bb07-38d950f613d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19100" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11arcsecx+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e6b5d85-0b53-580f-9520-6c33463231d5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions and Some Manipulations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d1fed6-2003-5f27-aae5-10563638a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284812} {:answer/id #uuid "4a505557-0c7c-5346-91d3-f43699946a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{arccos}\\,x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285476} {:answer/id #uuid "857d016b-947d-51cc-a94f-504ff730be03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286014} {:answer/id #uuid "95a187aa-f975-58a0-836c-3c543a56e0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{arctan}\\,x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286147} {:answer/id #uuid "ea53131b-47dd-5ccd-bab6-9d3aa84dd5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857d016b-947d-51cc-a94f-504ff730be03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286014} :answer-field/id #uuid "18a42158-518b-5329-b494-893d421c1e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a43a9d4-962c-592e-be6c-4c9a905c5e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-19102" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea29480-cac0-5cc3-8047-adc941fa21a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285655} {:answer/id #uuid "75622db7-e002-5c1b-9372-fa25bdc45120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285873} {:answer/id #uuid "b204700e-83e0-5a79-9f25-c452d7d023d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286416} {:answer/id #uuid "b826d348-d7ac-59b4-9416-af96b6d8c91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286469} {:answer/id #uuid "fc260a7d-4cf6-56c4-8c1c-5138b1ec36db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75622db7-e002-5c1b-9372-fa25bdc45120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285873} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd5b7c8-b33e-53f5-923e-d59cd403ae02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6f80f82-94f9-5bde-ac4d-44929ef66109" :question/math-academy-id "q-19110" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34f86b-faa2-5844-9680-3f6951dc4ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401807} {:answer/id #uuid "63214331-a811-5bf2-8cb7-4da5c8c1d678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{cot}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}csc⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401808} {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35c415-50b4-5005-8173-6121abbd643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}csc⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401810} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8e226f-a28c-5b1a-b7df-6f312bfd06a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aeb284-4f80-510f-8935-07a7e23c626b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4e083b-fcc9-5eb5-9a27-ac52f9a97afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1913" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x) \\\\  & =(-8{x}^{3})(cot⁡x)+(-2{x}^{4})(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x+2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9317eb-d1ce-5f42-b69b-a37e643b865b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Angle and Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f1a168-e459-5855-9f32-c7f995528292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285543} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ca20c-6282-53b7-a446-f76828bdd080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286224} {:answer/id #uuid "b1302f72-3e46-5025-ab0e-97b643675a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286407} {:answer/id #uuid "c07b1571-e4fd-5a58-a381-73bd15f01a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286548} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ff959-29d9-5163-867c-ef6c1827374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ff959-29d9-5163-867c-ef6c1827374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286643} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c576604-9cab-5f35-9107-e12da3cf2cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b19c031-650e-5cdc-af13-3ca3ed0d48bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19220" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=36$, $|\\,b\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={135}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-18\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $b:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{b}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}b\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-18\\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}b \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}b\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401814} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddc81bc-6b74-5b86-9aa6-68b7985e21ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401815} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2d52e0-5f90-57ca-a8a9-c0f4aaadbdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=10.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401816} {:answer/id #uuid "e34561e5-c74f-5726-99b2-cd744a8924ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401817} {:answer/id #uuid "eea394a2-6e5a-5394-a910-d19a50a2530e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401814} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab640647-5232-553a-be5a-08e8539221b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59ea1544-758d-5928-bf3f-4995dd8b61f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19299" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-t}+6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =-4{e}^{-2}+6\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2}+10 \\\\  & =9.458\\,658… \\\\  & ≈9.46\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9733cf69-5d70-514e-a80d-eadad1d41489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=6.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401821} {:answer/id #uuid "85286b89-c2a3-56c9-a420-e9d18189a069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401822} {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401823} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebd6114-b8d6-5140-a703-c4dd01c13274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401824} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2eab8c-d466-5781-91a5-1f6a93c6b3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401823} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc005a4-0a0a-5369-9aa9-e0229fc91626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df15b1d-5c6a-5a31-88c3-94f27234c33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19302" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=3.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t-2+\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =6-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =4.333\\,333… \\\\  & ≈4.33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07df5620-8117-5d74-b65a-568506c2ddda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284848} {:answer/id #uuid "4699fac1-5a8f-5b40-afed-7064158b1c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285437} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce9442e-a29f-597e-b2de-7761a1d30d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286222} {:answer/id #uuid "a22f372f-9504-584b-bc3e-87c16eaabc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286274} {:answer/id #uuid "e584a0a9-3825-5a55-8fc4-911388ec2a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a22f372f-9504-584b-bc3e-87c16eaabc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286274} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff436e5-f03c-513f-b406-812caeb45b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b26ce0-b29c-55b2-a912-d3113c434dd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-19393" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1d3691b-a1a5-5e81-bfe2-760939975d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401828} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebae067-1d98-5398-842f-52e5beff87e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401829} {:answer/id #uuid "1270a921-f7ad-5051-be4e-3a8d359de937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401830} {:answer/id #uuid "83d51c96-b38e-5a00-a86c-b8ab59d290d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401831} {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401832} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c042d42-f6d6-52fa-992c-350b4e969301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2001916-7ed2-504f-9a8e-d02fda09253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-19493" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb72661-686b-55b7-acd2-2a3bd4990839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284881} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa45466-3a87-5294-bb09-177ec594bc1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284925} {:answer/id #uuid "7da55036-6972-52f3-847b-a31f95df7852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285944} {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d61fb9-24f0-590c-861d-b0dc09f76a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} :answer-field/id #uuid "05333763-722c-5f4d-b56e-21e02dec7970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15807573-b299-5e98-85b6-db47d2fbe91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1952" :question/problem "Given that $y={2}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{u} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390355} {:answer/id #uuid "a0809cc0-8245-5717-8ded-90aaf0006a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390356} {:answer/id #uuid "de905a14-40b4-54a2-9966-938904217d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390357} {:answer/id #uuid "e79926dc-55a2-51bb-946e-9f9ee21d2e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390358} {:answer/id #uuid "703b11b7-a06a-596e-9766-2f893cbd71bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390355} :answer-field/id #uuid "4529ba01-864b-583e-ae2f-75a351bb79bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db8ef128-2717-5091-8870-85aa35ff95e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19532" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-12x+36)-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a180dc90-c6b9-577e-9eef-ed60ecafe4e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a58fd9-1eec-5255-a842-d9b344d5a32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285089} {:answer/id #uuid "244c711b-020d-50ea-a3c7-e3f53022f59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285105} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce9febb-24a9-5950-8f46-fb621aa09f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285362} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab55399-eaab-5bf2-b31d-0cc923874c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286205} {:answer/id #uuid "a252d0dc-972c-57ed-b476-d063056a314b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a252d0dc-972c-57ed-b476-d063056a314b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286275} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad78ce79-2c39-5ac5-96ff-eade1681f9f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00abbf43-3e03-55ab-b7aa-2f9087193808" :question/math-academy-id "q-19756" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=\\pi a+\\pi n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & =0+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\frac{3}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "733eab11-2a26-5402-8df5-bba50c9beaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a2ecca-4420-5a73-85e5-d8fd94cb2fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390362} {:answer/id #uuid "37f1f0b7-f7d0-59d9-b906-b1e9b3bc1438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390363} {:answer/id #uuid "1b801238-dd43-58b7-a037-5fb7adad9f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-15,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390364} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a830dc-88d1-5eec-a445-b8adc0a5469e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨15,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390365} {:answer/id #uuid "3abd8bb4-bf5c-5575-bf37-b9815f3df034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,5,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f1f0b7-f7d0-59d9-b906-b1e9b3bc1438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390363} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba37642-671c-52eb-9ccf-cac56d58f19b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765f0999-da93-5e44-b207-672576084830" :question/math-academy-id "q-19779" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,5,3⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5)i-(1\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =(0-15)i-(3-6)j+(5-0)k \\\\  & =⟨-15,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22766496-0abf-50a1-80d1-5c3aa17a5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285095} {:answer/id #uuid "2decc5e8-9169-5cef-a48f-c52612fb5bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285195} {:answer/id #uuid "68bba672-b656-58bd-ab7d-b6d8e1d686e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+7)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285750} {:answer/id #uuid "96c58f78-9e89-5c29-947e-3e0a3eea9a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(n+8)(n+9)}{n-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286158} {:answer/id #uuid "bd10a16d-830d-53c9-ba1c-682004537a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n+9}{n-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22766496-0abf-50a1-80d1-5c3aa17a5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285095} :answer-field/id #uuid "12acb7ca-836f-551c-8db4-207dc9c189b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3ef0665-6f2a-5dd9-af17-7da892c3b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-19882" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 9$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdf387f-3a0b-57c6-89ff-676228ded636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284894} {:answer/id #uuid "1b734ea2-a219-577a-8568-20df8a2e2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285031} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6ad601-4800-552a-b12e-a5b40856f5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285058} {:answer/id #uuid "4334150f-17b3-522c-8a54-1ba9d10b279e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285408} {:answer/id #uuid "c780a0ee-f288-5cb4-974a-df2c2b28d6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{m}{m+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cdf387f-3a0b-57c6-89ff-676228ded636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284894} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b22a75-1b58-5fe1-bc2a-8eed1972c5c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae781a4d-b2cf-5568-b0a5-f23d5dc664e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-19887" :question/problem "Given that $m\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(2m)!\\,(m+4)!}{(m+5)!\\,(2m-1)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+5)! & =(m+5)(m+4)! \\\\ 2m! & =2m(2m-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(m+4)!$ and $(2m-1)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2m)!\\,(m+4)!}{(m+5)!\\,(2m-1)!} & =\\frac{(2m)\\,\\cdot (2m-1)!\\cdot (m+4)!}{(m+5)\\cdot (m+4)!\\cdot (2m-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2m)\\,\\cdot \\cancel{(2m-1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(m+4)!}}{(m+5)\\cdot \\cancel{(m+4)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(2m-1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2m}{m+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e49e2595-62a1-595a-8df9-99541bccbdf5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Permutations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110abfe8-256f-586f-8c14-cdbacbf98aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284936} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8cbe92-7d56-57d4-ac77-8dcc36070b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285763} {:answer/id #uuid "75559a19-2283-512d-bed1-5bd115893a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285871} {:answer/id #uuid "7c34d0e6-2814-5d2f-be61-88b870668d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285933} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f7b5bb-d5a9-5ca5-9642-1dbe2e08c1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f7b5bb-d5a9-5ca5-9642-1dbe2e08c1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286271} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d5b268-f0c4-5b8e-8047-2fa1728600f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4641263-8717-5c07-a2d0-51003a352849" :question/math-academy-id "q-19899" :question/problem "Michael goes to a restaurant and orders a sandwich, a salad, and a muffin. In how many different orders can he decide to eat his food?" :question/worked-solution "There are $3$ foods, so there are $3!$ ways of ordering them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3! & =3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are 6 possible arrangements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1cac9a-d8ad-501d-b0a6-a0a1bebe9636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285345} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf88e45-9bac-51c6-a90f-f80f508a8095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285363} {:answer/id #uuid "46574c64-b2d9-52a5-ba20-5c4e8f687459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} {:answer/id #uuid "61a98581-4f83-59f8-a0f9-f6ec11524a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285684} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd903e1-1e62-5f4c-86df-6c375dc3361d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1cac9a-d8ad-501d-b0a6-a0a1bebe9636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b8bbd2-699b-5127-b6f0-02f2e8c72c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95f6f3a1-caa9-5902-b6fb-cb14398f3fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20027" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(1,2,1)$, $C(3,2,3).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300c21b4-1756-505c-8887-69d902ae3677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285224} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae0a167-e7b0-520f-9327-565bd22dde8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286063} {:answer/id #uuid "b624085b-014c-5e50-8b4b-f36fd36a52f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286450} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e671b4-92f5-5e79-bde0-a4e9c92974ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286575} {:answer/id #uuid "fb65991f-7c1a-5dcd-bc51-9c721902f012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b624085b-014c-5e50-8b4b-f36fd36a52f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286450} :answer-field/id #uuid "43229f82-3e46-5de5-bed3-19f2997ba66e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d05a20b-4c69-5e8d-bc26-f4e99fd04a4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-20054" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,-1,2⟩$, $b=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $c=⟨2,4,2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343abe13-b014-58c0-913d-2fc9f7a11d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285270} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3b1e45-a9c8-5b28-b558-ec4bc793eb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285496} {:answer/id #uuid "a878dcad-4ddf-585b-a29e-6f1bfa1aa273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286324} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e6bcc3-f1d7-59c6-953b-c03961aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286630} {:answer/id #uuid "fbedc400-7438-5567-8dfa-d1cfcb39be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbedc400-7438-5567-8dfa-d1cfcb39be79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287087} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a770db-2d9f-5df9-858e-0db4cb77fe94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae812812-92a4-5aa7-85f9-50adcd0043cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-20059" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b)$, if $3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c)=18\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $c$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $c$ and $a$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2871030f-535f-5bad-960f-2518315ab868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285139} {:answer/id #uuid "395873e5-15bd-53dd-8303-0a9cb00704ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285327} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9995e8-b44e-5b2b-a77c-68b604f74177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285501} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f44045-fc84-58ca-8c0c-ea930c16cece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286576} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4e1f02-b381-51f4-825c-11efa8be82bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2f44045-fc84-58ca-8c0c-ea930c16cece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286576} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ca935c-f519-5702-93c9-2f479ee86077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2054ccd0-385b-55a1-9e4b-6c29d327d7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-20061" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $3a\\cdot (6b\\times c)=90.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (6b\\times c) & =90 \\\\ 3\\cdot 6\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =90 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "471b8112-fe03-5559-8fd9-7d262e3aacb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285445} {:answer/id #uuid "51beac23-bf68-50c9-952d-653cee2aa6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285540} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac35f1-a93a-56ba-81c5-2d2d400444ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285834} {:answer/id #uuid "82f17c2a-2ee4-5365-bf0f-241c55acae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285989} {:answer/id #uuid "aa74e9c4-0cc5-5f42-a6c7-ce6192d80edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51beac23-bf68-50c9-952d-653cee2aa6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285540} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be7a502-2728-5180-92ba-ee5a5a51b7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe360508-b564-5f2e-8656-ff74569f459b" :question/math-academy-id "q-20214" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/6b/6bc5aed39e6421b8ff2416edb75dc888c8f38ae14f99b3d6e28d8e4c9302908f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e617f241-254b-5f70-9937-5883b6f28c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d4843e-1503-5b14-9ef4-6d9bd866b768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284994} {:answer/id #uuid "5f513476-ecb1-5481-9bb0-8cd729eba6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡xcot⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285660} {:answer/id #uuid "c159be5b-9ba9-50ec-90b5-6beba2ba516f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286558} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a909e4-a442-5f93-bb1c-8af5b5eeb86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5759735-03db-58c8-afc1-42a56314fd9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eefdd68-c49a-5b6b-9578-f46e438f3aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-2038" :question/problem "$\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =sec⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  & =-\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-(sec⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-sec⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "691b9107-a935-5965-b3ac-0f3a193ce9bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5c7599-29ce-5dfb-b9ba-7e6fb2bf85d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284920} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3de505-4af9-587f-bcca-65ed2365b867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285336} {:answer/id #uuid "5495a455-efc7-5ebe-b382-a6c7dce1ea64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285564} {:answer/id #uuid "f489821f-daaf-5cd9-bdf8-076fa189ac14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287030} {:answer/id #uuid "fd46554d-54cc-5fd1-8d36-7125d02933cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5495a455-efc7-5ebe-b382-a6c7dce1ea64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285564} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9e0856-cd52-5230-aead-a0e0ab0fa9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf5f9366-1ce3-5395-93b6-14a04149ebc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-20387" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $S$ into the matrix $T$, where\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that changes the $1$st  row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092170 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71da663-e7a4-5781-973c-5dc7e4deb83f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Horizontal Line and a Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a9c8d8-68c5-587e-838a-4a7f7a9f44bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284840} {:answer/id #uuid "269e3329-f871-5045-88f0-2b40179a9752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285128} {:answer/id #uuid "725d36f9-f189-56dd-98a0-db99c79bb457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285840} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b81c32-7857-5a5e-80e1-2d918aea9dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286425} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd63f8c-9c63-51fb-b9ec-35a75373c9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725d36f9-f189-56dd-98a0-db99c79bb457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285840} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0b2d2e-e7a6-5a1c-bd3e-245bfcd76f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc963679-7e9a-589a-a899-b98503fae964" :question/math-academy-id "q-20414" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f00d8bd6cbd1f113df7f023e161e4d79e2f4a81157d1ac9a8ab65e70b4959221.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc5590c-f5cf-5e21-9a2e-5e31772b304d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285183} {:answer/id #uuid "35a20bbd-e86d-5f6e-907f-c57e5562aefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285280} {:answer/id #uuid "531f071e-da47-5a18-876a-8d7ce3863d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285552} {:answer/id #uuid "80ddc038-3db7-5447-8486-3d32260a66f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285973} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a429df-36c3-50d3-a2b3-d9bf1a44b2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a429df-36c3-50d3-a2b3-d9bf1a44b2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286477} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd9a91d-854c-5377-a157-19bd44fd94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4f66a0-1cfd-52d9-be40-ea47c3b77cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-20435" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=\\frac{1}{2}a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae30bba-5530-5871-9ea8-4343da788a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285617} {:answer/id #uuid "84ac8a3e-9db1-5899-a94c-f1d8a6b9f8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286009} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcb2df3-855d-5d8c-a44e-0d9a80e2da45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286231} {:answer/id #uuid "b551c659-6a03-59d6-a275-55b8eeb6c65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286438} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0025fc-ffa4-5c23-9ca1-fc484cd0db28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0025fc-ffa4-5c23-9ca1-fc484cd0db28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286929} :answer-field/id #uuid "99080949-e49d-5bbc-ac7f-90d7689382a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a5ebda6-bb81-57c5-aa1e-8e3aa3418e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-20452" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2-2(-3)=-2+6=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4)+3(-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-8+9=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808894a0-c611-5eb7-bd57-7f7029f0ef0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals Involving Arctangent Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c9656-50c7-5f2f-a75f-303286f63722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285192} {:answer/id #uuid "48d663a7-0d4d-5e1a-829c-702875e8fa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285457} {:answer/id #uuid "71ebd32d-bc3e-5795-a3f3-f25bd368644f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285836} {:answer/id #uuid "adc96207-8393-5f0a-a5c6-a03c114c44e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286374} {:answer/id #uuid "f74274cd-f977-54ba-8234-27fbf9f752cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f74274cd-f977-54ba-8234-27fbf9f752cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287052} :answer-field/id #uuid "db798c01-6b38-52e4-923a-3271058a473d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86974777-e957-5007-90f0-8770ec52a454" :question/math-academy-id "q-20502" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/f5/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b6f110-fbc4-5322-8a05-6366ff4c13c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395771} {:answer/id #uuid "e6302263-6554-5d2f-bdfe-094ceb26b042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395772} {:answer/id #uuid "337c9891-a990-5356-ae53-39e28e060932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,4,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395773} {:answer/id #uuid "12ab6864-c814-589e-8f72-328eeae44af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨13,-4,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395774} {:answer/id #uuid "fa67123d-ee59-563d-90e5-5d86c85b1243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-11,4,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "337c9891-a990-5356-ae53-39e28e060932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,4,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395773} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a758e48-dc27-560b-a0af-fd01b7ea572b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3437106d-95df-5058-942b-4f64c5179bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20512" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & 2x+3y+4z=6 \\\\  & 3x+3y+4z=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & y=4 \\\\  & z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=4$ and $z=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(4)+2(-4)=1\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5,y=4$, and $z=-4.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨5,4,-4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15719692-f572-52e2-92ef-7c37695a20ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284977} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9ba6d-8ad5-5004-96ed-80c84894925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286091} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5c7bb0-6725-548b-b664-64d4ff92c96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286504} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbdd67-60e8-56e1-96f6-8012386dd066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286742} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e5b59d-43dc-5f6c-8987-626af7040c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5c7bb0-6725-548b-b664-64d4ff92c96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286504} :answer-field/id #uuid "28841267-b70d-52db-841b-c4f0d84407a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e202a5f2-1485-54d2-ad75-6c42b9340de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20524" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8326c365-38e8-509f-ba26-6de3d26a5411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390376} {:answer/id #uuid "f0893938-c4dc-5b69-ac7a-20d7546db3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390377} {:answer/id #uuid "98de7d9c-ac96-50b3-a7cd-2e1f0a084bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390378} {:answer/id #uuid "5d46c30a-315a-5f3c-9e1c-febc548b0a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390379} {:answer/id #uuid "c961a688-8a1a-5a32-8996-3f5dbb760b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0893938-c4dc-5b69-ac7a-20d7546db3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2efbab-ca3d-5dfb-bdbf-f29fc5964e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b0ab2ea-5bc8-5361-b6e4-ca31cdf1350a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20539" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7294399b-b139-5ee6-824c-5e28e4fc49ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a91e48e3-62ec-5edb-bcf7-03d4a5806e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390383} {:answer/id #uuid "5461c0a1-245b-5870-8efa-189453e52726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390384} {:answer/id #uuid "0327ce18-3178-58df-99a3-60dc77f9fed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390385} {:answer/id #uuid "fabd327a-4307-5856-a87b-ef1b6f25459d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390386} {:answer/id #uuid "91ef62e9-3380-521c-8cbb-38d749ba6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91e48e3-62ec-5edb-bcf7-03d4a5806e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390383} :answer-field/id #uuid "13da932e-9b2a-5e64-abc3-8e5afbbc90ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f45c2e0-9678-5cdd-b7f1-5fd5cc96fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-20590" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=0 \\\\  & y+2z=0 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} {:answer/id #uuid "68cdc6da-3b7a-57fc-8b9f-cd3018eea1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285751} {:answer/id #uuid "6d97721e-a156-5007-a747-5f849a77796c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798} {:answer/id #uuid "888fbc31-5edc-5fc6-ba9c-9dca4d6a73b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286040} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4d35d0-edf6-5b04-974a-d41a0d358490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3447b210-752f-58bc-ae21-6727e68e220b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285271} :answer-field/id #uuid "43979b98-c797-55a1-8e56-30f10ae875af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15a1f53-63be-5a09-b32e-0a9e0c9c95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-20700" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463bda05-30a6-5f43-9fe8-a6e053505a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285430} {:answer/id #uuid "62a7dfb9-8e2d-5329-a19a-e69dfd7a639a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285691} {:answer/id #uuid "8562f479-b79c-5bfc-9052-d2dd00d037d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286013} {:answer/id #uuid "8f788f95-a115-5ce9-ab9e-1c9905482ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286097} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ec5508-5b77-5348-9a3d-570f81c31d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8562f479-b79c-5bfc-9052-d2dd00d037d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286013} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e36243-4323-5e17-ae45-dac66122d031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b7327d0-ac78-5202-8dce-774362ca097e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20701" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1318686a66856057fb04027ac52ff20266e628a498458b283ee93df1e4294eb.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y={e}^{-|x|}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand ${e}^{-|x|}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0234256b-4322-512e-92e1-be78bf6561a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284806} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4341c5-a3c5-51c6-96a4-fdaf4711e8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284897} {:answer/id #uuid "ae40fe8f-38e8-5ed6-9c7d-2ed99ccf3fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286379} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ca9b83-4a3a-557d-a4a4-e2331cb89ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286589} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ffcac9-a482-5c55-8c94-0e7d4ace356c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4ca9b83-4a3a-557d-a4a4-e2331cb89ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286589} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50f08d1-2237-56c5-a8d0-686d44fe95f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f51d9ec2-7cd0-5c01-b675-e7bf731d23db" :question/math-academy-id "q-20804" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{u}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fd7882-e12b-5d1e-bda2-674211a5aad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395778} {:answer/id #uuid "61e0f80f-69b1-578c-857a-82bc0682835a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395779} {:answer/id #uuid "26a77c96-4bef-5e82-8889-1c4a9ce7d523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395780} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c50146-0d7c-501d-bf37-4d8d838c3cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d7a736-a513-51fb-b585-d99caa2d0531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b1f64f-9248-57a2-a310-050eb4c80ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20849" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9371d5e2bb921b4f5e58423bbce492f2b555122ee42bd861040b57d01070353.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-1\\le t<1,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8t}{2}=4t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =4(0) \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401845} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf43324-c63f-59fe-a56a-d4b9b0b7048c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369973} {:answer/id #uuid "d31fde31-428f-55b0-b1d5-e09807d05e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369974} {:answer/id #uuid "1e516990-d1af-5886-b235-bc549708f3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369975} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb22e6-abf7-597f-994c-d38d0bf975fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369976} {:answer/id #uuid "04436aad-8c02-5516-88b3-fa9120784970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31fde31-428f-55b0-b1d5-e09807d05e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369974} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbdfe1c-d664-50dc-a125-12450a2859e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14405449-2d85-5d75-b26c-28e2f262eedb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20869" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9dd871-8794-5940-9040-b45ed2aefb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285178} {:answer/id #uuid "37603a20-d773-5b54-9dfb-a98db3280a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285306} {:answer/id #uuid "9631e71c-c418-589d-aaaa-92ecad6c3df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286151} {:answer/id #uuid "e87a202e-4f0f-552e-a281-207262f89759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286915} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb99fe-c43e-5a78-a679-aa67521ebfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e87a202e-4f0f-552e-a281-207262f89759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286915} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea5ef19-3af1-5414-9744-b16864d7b309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "826720bb-562f-595c-b16e-c49b0073360a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20891" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
